.TH SH-SESSION 1
.SH NAME
sh-session \- drive and describe a session from the shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load session
.PP
.B session script
.I recording
.br
.B session play
.I recording
.br
.B session type
.I text
.br
.B session key
.I name
.br
.B session click
.I x y
.RI [ button ]
.br
.B session doubleclick
.I x y
.RI [ button ]
.br
.B session drag
.I x0 y0 x1 y1
.RI [ button ]
.br
.B session move
.I x y
.br
.B session wait
.I seconds
.br
.B session pause
.RI [ seconds ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Session
turns a recording made by
.B INFERNO_INPUT_RECORD
(see
.IR emu (1))
into shell, and runs it.
.PP
.B Script
prints the recording as a script.
.B Play
runs one directly, honouring the waits it contains.
Timing shorter than a wait is not reproduced \(em actions inside a run go as
fast as the writes do \(em which is part of what
.I approximate
means below.
The remaining verbs are the actions themselves, which is what a printed script
is made of.
.SH EXAMPLE
A session recorded by typing at it:
.IP
.EX
% session script /sess.in
# 7 actions, 18.405s
session type 'echo hello'
session wait 1.997
session key Return
session wait 1.978
session type 'quitall'
session wait 1.364
session key Return
.EE
.PP
Thirty-seven key presses and releases, said in a way that can be read, edited
and run.
Running it against a fresh
.IR wm (1)
delivers the same nineteen keystrokes the recording captured, in the same
order.
.SS "Stopping in the middle"
.B Pause
waits for a line on the console.
With a number it waits that long instead, which is what an unattended script
wants: standard input here is
.BR #c/cons ,
not whatever the host redirected, so a bare
.B pause
in a script with nobody at the console waits for ever.
While it waits the system underneath is running normally, so the machine can be
used \(em windows opened, things typed \(em and then the script carries on.
.IP
.EX
session type 'echo one'
session key Return
session pause
session type 'echo two'
session key Return
.EE
.PP
There is no protocol for this and there does not need to be one.
A script that has stopped is a shell that has not been given its next line, and
nothing about the rest of the system is suspended while that is true.
That is the reason for making the recording into shell rather than into a
player with its own controls.
.SH "TWO WAYS TO REPLAY, AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME"
.B INFERNO_INPUT_REPLAY
feeds the recorded events back below every bind, at the times they originally
arrived.
It is faithful \(em same events, same order, same gaps \(em and it cannot be
interrupted, because the real keyboard and mouse are ignored for the duration.
.PP
This feeds
.I actions
through
.B /dev/keyboard
and
.BR /dev/pointerin .
It is approximate: everything is re-timed, a window resize cannot be performed
at all, and a pointer position means whatever is at those coordinates now.
It can be stopped anywhere, edited, and run in pieces.
.PP
Use the first to reproduce a session, the second to demonstrate one or to take
it over.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/session.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sh (1),
.IR emu (1),
.IR session (2),
.IR pointer (3)
.SH BUGS
A scripted session cannot be recorded: the injection paths deliberately do not
record, so there is no record, edit, record again.
See
.IR session (2).
.PP
A timed
.B pause
is a plain sleep rather than a console read raced against a timer, because the
losing reader would stay blocked on the console and a blocked process keeps
.I emu
alive after everything else has finished.
So a timed pause cannot be cut short by pressing return.
.PP
.B Session resize
parses and then refuses: the host owns the window size.
.PP
Coordinates are absolute and are not scaled to the window, so a script recorded
against one window size clicks the wrong things in another.
.PP
Nothing checks that a script matches the system it is run against.
It types and clicks where it is told, and if the windows are elsewhere it will
say so to whatever is there.
